# How many machinery entry points a program can CALL, and a digest of which.
#
# The register in vm_forbidden.golden counts what a program can reach and
# WRITE, and answers none. That is true and it is not the whole question. An
# operator is not a writable object, so a sealed, read-only object graph
# answers none while an operator stands that installs a replacement for any
# part of it. Calling such an operator reaches what writing the object would
# have reached, by a route the writable count cannot see.
#
# The roots are the ones a program actually holds: systemdict, userdict and
# globaldict, which need nothing, and internaldict, whose password the
# specification publishes (PLRM 8). internaldict is therefore a root a program
# holds, not a place where things are out of reach: a name moved into it is as
# callable as it was, by two tokens any program can write.
#
# THERE ARE NO NAMES HERE, AND THERE MUST NOT BE. A list of the entry points a
# program can still call is a map for whoever would like to call them, and this
# tree and its build logs are published. The count says which way the set moved
# and the digest says whether it moved at all. Someone working on it adds a
# print to the walk in tests/machinery_reachable_test.ps locally.
#
# Each count is a RATCHET: it may fall, it may not rise, and the pair is
# rewritten in the commit that moves it.
#
# nopassword -- reachable from systemdict, userdict and globaldict in a
# sandboxed run, which is the shipped default. The two are the private
# dictionary and the scratch dictionary, both named in systemdict because an
# operator implemented in PostScript reaches them there.
nopassword 2 71
#
# nosandbox -- the same walk in a run started with --no-sandbox, which keeps
# the four file-permission entry points in systemdict that the sandbox
# removes. A separate line because they are absent by configuration, not by
# construction, and a register that recorded only the sandboxed run would call
# that an absence.
nosandbox 6 28023
#
# password -- everything internaldict holds, counted whole rather than by the
# dot the tree marks machinery with, because a machinery name carrying no dot
# is exactly the one a census keyed on dots would not see. This number is the
# work: it is the population that is reachable today and should not be, and
# nothing about the shape of the object graph brings it down. Only removing an
# entry point, or refusing it in a shipped run the way the census operators
# already refuse, moves it.
password 141 4857
